  "success": true,
  "output": "## Summary\n\nThis is a small Next.js 14 app-router site (no database, no ORM) — a parallax/Three.js landing page plus a localStorage-backed \"progress\" feature. Two of your five categories (N+1 queries, \"unnecessary re-renders\" in the classic list-of-components sense) mostly don't apply to the code that exists today, and the parts that do exist are already well engineered (proper cleanup in every hook, GPU resource disposal, frameloop gating in the WebGL scene, fetch-level caching on the one external API call). I'll flag what's real rather than invent problems.\n\n### 1. N+1 queries — not applicable, but one caching nuance\n\nThere's no database. The only network call is `app/api/air-quality/route.js`, a single server-side proxy to WAQI, and it's **not yet wired to any UI component** (grepped for `/api/air-quality` usage — none found outside the route itself).\n\nOne real nuance worth fixing now, before it's wired up: the response always sets `Cache-Control: no-store`, even for successful payloads.\n\n```js\n// app/api/air-quality/route.js\nconst json = (body, status = 200) =>\n  Response.json(body, { status, headers: { 'Cache-Control': 'no-store' } });\n```\n\nThe upstream `fetch` is cached server-side via `next: { revalidate: 900 }`, but the HTTP response Next.js sends to the browser/CDN is always uncacheable — so every visitor re-hits your serverless function even during the 15-minute revalidation window. Differentiate by status:\n\n```js\nconst json = (body, status = 200) => {\n  const cache = body.status === 'ok'\n    ? `public, s-maxage=${AIR_QUALITY.revalidateSeconds}, stale-while-revalidate=60`\n    : 'no-store'; // keep error/unconfigured states honest and uncached\n  return Response.json(body, { status, headers: { 'Cache-Control': cache } });\n};\n```\n\nWhen you build the client UI for this, use SWR/React Query keyed by `city` with a `staleTime` matching 900s — otherwise multiple components rendering the same city will each fire their own `fetch`.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders — one real coupling issue\n\n`PlanLink` in `components/layout/Header.js` reads the **entire** store:\n\n```js\nfunction PlanLink({ className, onClick }) {\n  const { plan } = useHavoState();   // subscribes to ALL of state\n  const open = plan.filter((p) => !p.completedAt).length;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n`features/progress/store.js`'s reducer returns a new top-level object on *every* dispatch (`{ ...state, quiz: {...} }`, `{ ...state, proposals: [...] }`, etc.). Since `useSyncExternalStore` compares the whole snapshot by reference, `PlanLink` re-renders — and recomputes the `.filter()` — whenever *anything* changes: recording a quiz score, adding a proposal, toggling a checklist, changing the weekly goal. It's rendered twice (desktop nav + mobile menu), so that's two unnecessary re-renders per unrelated dispatch, for the lifetime of the page.\n\nFix: add selector support to the store so components can subscribe to a slice instead of the whole object.\n\n```js\n// features/progress/store.js\nexport function useHavoState(selector = (s) => s) {\n  return useSyncExternalStore(\n    subscribe,\n    () => selector(getSnapshot()),\n    () => selector(INITIAL_STATE),\n  );\n}\n```\n\n```js\n// Header.js\nfunction PlanLink({ className, onClick }) {\n  const open = useHavoState((s) => s.plan.filter((p) => !p.completedAt).length);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThis also sets you up for the dashboard components you haven't built yet (`computeBadges`, `computeStreak`, etc. in `features/progress/progress.js`) — without selectors, any component that calls those derived-data functions directly in its render body will redo all of them (badge progress, streak scan, best-week scan) on every unrelated dispatch too. Wrap those with `useMemo` keyed on the relevant slice once you wire up the UI:\n\n```js\nconst badges = useMemo(() => computeBadges(state), [state.history, state.quiz, state.proposals, state.weeklyGoal]);\n```\n\nEverything else I checked — `Hero`, `HeroScene`/`HeroVisual`, `Toaster`, `Sheet`, `ConfirmDialog`, `Counter`, `ChoiceGroup` — correctly keeps scroll/pointer-driven values as motion values (not React state), so they animate without triggering React re-renders at all. That's the right pattern; no changes needed there.\n\n### 3. Memory leaks — none found\n\nEvery effect I checked cleans up correctly:\n- `useInViewport`, `useActiveSection`: `IntersectionObserver.disconnect()` on unmount.\n- `useDialogBehavior`: removes the keydown listener, cancels the `requestAnimationFrame`, restores `body.style.overflow`/`paddingRight` and focus.\n- `HeroVisual`: `pointermove` listener removed on unmount.\n- `HeroScene`'s `Globe`: disposes every Three.js resource (geometries, materials, the canvas texture) in a cleanup effect keyed on `resources` — this correctly runs both on unmount *and* when quality tier changes (low/high swap), which is the easy place to leak GPU memory and isn't leaking here.\n- `features/feedback/toast.js`: clears the previous `setTimeout` before starting a new one.\n\n### 4. Redundant computation — one concrete, low-cost fix\n\n`recommendActions` in `features/actions/finder.js` builds full \"caution\" message strings (`.map().join()`) for **every** action that passes the hard filters, even though only the top 6 (`MAX_RESULTS`) are ever returned:\n\n```js\ncautions.push(\n  `Лучше подходит для мест: ${action.settings.map((s) => SETTING_LABELS[s].toLowerCase()).join(', ')}`,\n);\n```\n\nWith `data/actions.js` holding dozens of entries, this is cheap today, but it's wasted string-building for entries that get discarded by `.slice(0, MAX_RESULTS)` after sorting. If this list grows, defer caution-string construction until after the sort/slice:\n\n```js\nresults.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score || a.action.difficulty - b.action.difficulty);\nreturn results.slice(0, MAX_RESULTS).map((r) => ({ ...r, cautions: buildCautions(r.action, answers) }));\n```\n\nSame advice applies to `calculateImpact`'s `series` array (`features/impact/impact.js`) and the `computeBadges`/`computeStreak`/`bestWeekCount` trio in `progress.js` — all pure and cheap at current data sizes, but none of them are memoized at the call site, because **none of them are called from a component yet** (no `ActionFinder`, `ImpactCalculator`, or `ProgressDashboard` component exists under `components/` — only the data/logic layer is built). When you wire them up, memoize with `useMemo` keyed on actual inputs (answers, calculator inputs, store slice) rather than recomputing on every render.\n\n### Bottom line\n\nThe codebase is in good shape — the one substantive fix is giving `features/progress/store.js` selector support before more components start consuming it, since that's what will determine whether the (not-yet-built) progress dashboard re-renders/recomputes efficiently or not.\n",
